WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.269 --> 00:00:14.670
What it do yo? This week we need
some freakin’ CONDOMS with One Hundred Years

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:18.849
of Solitude by Gabriel Garcia Marquez.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:26.940
One night, stone cold playa Jose
Arcadio Buendia has a trippy dream bout a

00:00:26.950 --> 00:00:32.050
swanked-out city that got mirrors for walls.
The name that ragin through his head? Macondo.

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:37.500
It’s at dat moment that he tells the other
homies in posse: “Yo hold up. We settin

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:41.539
up shop here.” And thus, Macondo is born.

00:00:41.539 --> 00:00:45.829
So he settles there with his biddy
Ursula Iguaran where they get freaky on the

00:00:45.829 --> 00:00:51.149
reg and she pops out a swoel batch of kids.
But Jose don’t pay them much mind. Instead,

00:00:51.149 --> 00:00:56.600
fool like to isolate himself in his stuffy-ass
digs and get geeked up bout life’s mysteries.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:01.489
At first Macando was a fly spot
where nobody got beef and people ain’t even

00:01:01.489 --> 00:01:06.329
die. But eventually, the civilized world starts
gettin all up in their turf and a civil war

00:01:06.329 --> 00:01:07.189
busts out.

00:01:07.189 --> 00:01:12.130
As the text go on, we kickin it
with Jose Arcadio Buendia’s kids, then their

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:17.759
kids, then their kids- 7 generations of bustas
with basically the same name.

00:01:17.759 --> 00:01:20.240
But it ain’t only the names they be
sharin.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.530
Over time, we seein the Buendias
and baby Buendias doin the same things over

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:29.189
and over- makin babies with family members,
isolating themselves, getting obsessed with

00:01:29.189 --> 00:01:31.609
mysteries, I could go on playa.

00:01:31.609 --> 00:01:35.389
Eventually, the railroad tracks
make it to Macando and things starts gettin

00:01:35.389 --> 00:01:40.779
real dirty- a bunch o shysti capitalists set
up a banana plantation and straight up ICE

00:01:40.779 --> 00:01:45.459
homies that refuse to hustle. Between dat
and the Buendias’ fatal habits, we seein

00:01:45.459 --> 00:01:47.849
Macondo fall straight to sh**.

00:01:47.849 --> 00:01:51.659
Eventually, the town breathes its
last breath when a big-ass hurricane drops

00:01:51.659 --> 00:01:57.439
in to town. Just then, the great, great, GREAT
grandson of Jose Arcadio Buendia translates

00:01:57.439 --> 00:02:01.520
a secret code spittin that errrything that
happened to his family was already written

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:07.689
100 years ago. Da hell?

00:02:07.689 --> 00:02:11.400
Now listen up and let me school you
on this book’s title, son. One of the PHATTEST

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:15.510
themes Marquez slangin up in here is the theme
of solitude.

00:02:15.510 --> 00:02:19.260
From the moment yo ass get
yanked straight from yo mama’s oo- wee,

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:23.970
you in this game all by yo- self. The rest
of yo life becomes a process of realizin yo

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:28.650
separated from the rest of the hood, hustlin’
relentless to try to fix it, then cruisin

00:02:28.650 --> 00:02:34.570
down that last lonely road: death. And each
one of them Buendia playas reppin they own

00:02:34.570 --> 00:02:35.060
solitude.

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:40.170
For example, our boy Jose Arcadio
Buendia’s always dippin out of his regular

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:44.210
life cuz he jonezin for somethin’ new 
and fresh.

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:58.430
But eventually Jose realize dat the
so called new and fresh ain’t nothin but

00:02:58.430 --> 00:03:03.260
an illusion. On the real, life is a cycle
where the same mess happen over and over.

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:07.320
You can peep dat theme reflected in
the dankest symbol of this book: mirrors.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:12.630
Images of mirrors bein slung all up in this
text like Marquez don’t even give a fu**!

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:18.880
Jose see mirrors in dreams, the twins Aureliano
Segundo and Jose Arcadio Segundo act like

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:23.100
mirrors to one anotha, and Macando even called
a city of mirrors.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:26.940
And in this crazy-ass city, the
Buendia family is like a house of mirrors.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:32.850
Cuz errything that Jose Aracadio Buendia do
reflects on all 7 generations of dem Beundias.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:38.240
Not only are all dem cats repeating the same
stupid sh** over and over, but it all happening

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:43.960
at the same time. Marquez’s style preachin
that time, place, character- none of dat mess

00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:48.720
mean nothin. We’re just in this big- ass
flow where time don’t matter at all, cuz

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:51.090
errything and errybody bleeds together.

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:54.580
But by the end of the book, the
idea that time don’t matter gets flipped

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:59.190
right on it’s ass: when Aureliano finally
translates da one hundred year old code, he

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:03.810
realize errything bout the Buendias- including
his own death, was already in the cards.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:29.479
Gonna have to reflect on that
reflection, yo. But don’t worry playboy:

00:04:29.479 --> 00:04:34.719
It’s also foretold that you gonna hit that
SUBSCRIBE button OVER AND OVER AGAIN. Catch

00:04:34.719 --> 00:04:36.550
y’all playas later. Peace.

